    "chunk_text": "The Meethotamulla judgment and what it really means: community voices from 2018\n\nOn 31 March, 2026, Sri Lanka\u2019s Supreme Court delivered a judgment in which the court found that the Colombo Municipal Council had violated the fundamental rights of residents by continuing to dump garbage at Meethotamulla\n\nThe picture here is from the present-day Meethotamulla garbage dump which collapsed on 14 April 2017, killing 32 people. It looks almost like a forest now. Today people in the area have even used the hill to build replicas of Mihinthalaya and Siripada mountain-like structures from time to time during Vesak and Poson festivals. This former garbage dump stands as a quiet but powerful symbol of the environmental injustices faced by some segments of our society.\n\nOn 31 March, 2026, Sri Lanka\u2019s Supreme Court delivered a judgment in which the court found that the Colombo Municipal Council had violated the fundamental rights of residents by continuing to dump garbage at Meethotamulla.\n\nThat judgment made me reach for my field notebook. In 2018, I spoke with some of the people who were part of the social movement against the Meethotamulla garbage dump for a case study on environmental injustices. I want to share some of their stories and experiences here, because I believe the Supreme Court judgment becomes more meaningful for citizens when understood in the context of what those people lived through, the questions they continuously raised, and about their desire for justice.\n\nSince the early 2010s, the garbage dump had severely impacted the daily lives of the surrounding community. People raised concerns not only about the volume of garbage but also its nature. Waste from butcher shops, chicken feathers, and animal remains were a common sight around the area. Crows would carry scraps from the dump and scatter them across the neighbourhood. Residents described finding animal remains left on treetops by crows, which would rot, produce worms, and fall on people walking below."
