    "chunk_size_bytes": 1508,
    "chunk_text": "In a career lasting 46 years, divided equally between Sri Lanka and my adopted \u2018fatherland\u2019, I have served my patients going beyond the call of my duty. Not that I have not made mistakes, after all I am human, but have always learned from my mistakes. What concerns me increasingly is the lack of care and concern of the younger members of my profession and it is no surprise that the public no longer consider ours to be a noble profession.\n\nPatients waiting in a state-run hospital during a recent GMOA strike\n\nBritish Medical Association (BMA) is a rich and powerful organisation, trade union activities being a part of its remit, and calls itself \u201cthe trade union and professional body for doctors in the UK.\u201d It has a membership close to 200,000 and has several committees looking after individual groups, \u201cJunior Doctors Committee\u201d being one of the biggest and most powerful.\n\nDemanding a salary increase of over 35%, on the basis that annual salary increases granted have fallen cumulatively behind inflation, Junior doctors went on strike in March 2023. As they had no progress with the then Conservative government, they had another strike in 2023 to coincide with the Conservative party annual conference which helped Labour in getting 411 out of 631 seats in the UK parliament in the July 2024 election. They were adequately rewarded as the incoming Labour government gave junior doctors an unprecedented 28.9% pay rise and allowed a name change to \u2018Resident Doctors\u2019, in addition."
