    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "University of Sri Jayewardenepura Chemistry graduates set sights on global research and industry\n\nThe Department of Chemistry at the University of Sri Jayewardenepura has celebrated a new cohort of high-performing graduates, underscoring its role as a leading pipeline for scientific talent feeding both international research institutions and Sri Lanka\u2019s industrial sector.\n\nAt a ceremony held this week, academic staff hosted 37 graduates from its flagship Chemistry Honours and Industrial Chemistry degree programs, selected from a larger intake of over 400 students, who are now preparing to transition into research and professional careers.\n\nThe department, one of the largest in the university\u2019s science faculty, offers an internationally competitive curriculum that has consistently enabled students to secure research assistantships and postgraduate placements at top-ranked universities overseas. At the same time, its industry-oriented training has supplied Sri Lanka with a steady stream of skilled graduates for sectors ranging from manufacturing to applied sciences.\n\nFaculty members noted that graduates from the program regularly progress to leadership roles in both academia and industry, reflecting the department\u2019s emphasis on combining theoretical knowledge with practical and research-based training.\n\nFormer Heads of Department Prof. Ajith Abeysekera and Prof. Samitha Deraniyagala were also present at the event, sharing insights and encouragement with the graduating class as they prepare for the next phase of their careers.\n\nThe occasion, marked by a celebratory atmosphere, highlighted the department\u2019s broader contribution to building Sri Lanka\u2019s scientific capacity, while maintaining strong links with the global research community.\n\nAcademic staff extended their best wishes to the graduates, expressing confidence that they would continue to make meaningful contributions to the field of chemistry and beyond, both locally and internationally."
