    "chunk_text": "Forever Hero Herath: Remembering a JVP Sahodaraya on the 55th Anniversary\n\nKrakow, POLAND \u2013 This year, Easter Sunday on April 5 was a deeply meaningful and reflective day for me. As I contemplated the solemn stillness of the crucifixion of Jesus Christ and the hope of His resurrection, my thoughts travelled far beyond the Catholic churches and quiet streets of Poland\u2014back to the rice fields, dusty roads, and restless dreams of my childhood in the medieval capital of Polonnaruwa, Sri Lanka.\n\nOne memory, in particular, remains vivid. It was exactly 55 years ago\u2014April 5, 1971. On that Monday morning, as sunlight filtered through the coconut trees, my geography teacher, Tudor Luzon, and I set out on our usual walk to Palugasdamana Maha Vidyalaya. I was just two days shy of my twelfth birthday\u2014a sixth-grade student unaware that history had already erupted before sunrise.\n\nCoordinated attacks on police stations across the island had begun, marking the start of the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) insurrection. A passing police patrol stopped us abruptly and instructed us to put our hands up and return home. An island-wide curfew had been imposed under the government of Sirimavo Bandaranaike.\n\nThis year, the JVP-led government under President Anura Kumara Dissanayake commemorated both the 1971 uprising and the 1988\u201389 insurrection\u2014honoring the fallen youth as \u201cforever heroes.\u201d Among them, one face remains indelibly etched in my memory: my friend and dear sahodaraya\u2014H. B. Herath of Anuradhapura.\n\nWe called each other sahodaraya\u2014brother, comrade. It was more than a political label; it reflected a shared struggle and a shared sense of purpose."
