    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "This year, the JVP-led government under President Anura Kumara Dissanayake commemorated both the 1971 uprising and the 1988\u201389 insurrection\u2014honoring the fallen youth as \u201cforever heroes.\u201d Among them, one face remains indelibly etched in my memory: my friend and dear sahodaraya\u2014H. B. Herath of Anuradhapura.\n\nWe called each other sahodaraya\u2014brother, comrade. It was more than a political label; it reflected a shared struggle and a shared sense of purpose.\n\nHerath came from the dry plains of Anuradhapura\u2014the island\u2019s first capital for over 1,200 years\u2014while Polonnaruwa, my home, was the later rice-exporting capital. Today, both stand as UNESCO World Heritage Sites. We were sons of the soil, shaped by agriculture, monsoon uncertainty, and the quiet resilience of rural life grounded in Buddhist culture\u2014though I was born to a Buddhist mother and a Catholic father.\n\nWe understood what it meant to grow up far from the privileges of Colombo elites\u2014to study under difficult conditions, to aspire against the odds, and to believe that education was the only bridge between hardship and hope.\n\nYears later, in 1979, I entered the University of Sri Jayewardenepura (USJ) after completing my academic year 1978-79 in the United States through an American Field Service (AFS) scholarship at Perham High School in Minnesota. After I returned to Sri Lanka, my path crossed with Herath on the Sri Jayewardenepura University sports ground. The campus was alive with sports enthusiasm, political energy, intellectual ferment, and youthful idealism.\n\nWhile I pursued student leadership\u2014as founding president of the World University Service chapter and secretary of the University Sports Council\u2014Herath emerged as a prominent figure among JVP-affiliated student leaders.\n\nH. B. Herath (right) and Amarasiri Samaranayake (middle) at a protest against the White Paper on Education"
