    "chunk_size_bytes": 2014,
    "chunk_text": "While I pursued student leadership\u2014as founding president of the World University Service chapter and secretary of the University Sports Council\u2014Herath emerged as a prominent figure among JVP-affiliated student leaders.\n\nH. B. Herath (right) and Amarasiri Samaranayake (middle) at a protest against the White Paper on Education\n\nOn campus, Herath had a commanding presence\u2014physically imposing yet gentle in spirit. He reminded me of Mahasona, one of the ten legendary warriors of King Dutugemunu. On the volleyball court, Herath was disciplined and composed; on the badminton court, I was far less graceful. But in conversation, he was thoughtful, warm, and driven by conviction.\n\nIn 1981, when Education Minister Ranil Wickremesinghe of the governing United National Party (UNP) introduced the controversial White Paper on Education, Herath and the Inter-University Students Federation (IUSF) played a central role in mobilizing opposition. Drawing on strong connections with the JVP and the broader student movement, they organized mass protests around the country.\n\nIn January, thousands of students, monks, and trade unionists gathered near Parliament in Colombo. Their slogan\u2014\u201dKolambata Kiri, Apata Kekiri\u201d or \u201cMilk for Colombo, melon for us\u201d\u2014captured the deep inequality between urban elites and rural students. This was more than a policy dispute; it was a struggle over fairness, access, and the future of students like Herath and me.\n\nIn February 1982, Herath invited me to a JVP rally at Hyde Park in support of Rohana Wijeweera, the leader of the movement. That day, I witnessed Wijeweera\u2019s powerful oratory for the first time\u2014magnetic and uncompromising. I glanced at Herath during the speech; his eyes reflected both intensity and hope, as if he truly believed a just Sri Lanka was within reach.\n\nH. B. Herath, a 1980 graduate of the University of Sri Jayewardenepura (photo was taken 1975)\n\nThe sustained protests eventually forced the UNP government to withdraw the White Paper."
