    "chunk_size_bytes": 1714,
    "chunk_text": "In February 1982, Herath invited me to a JVP rally at Hyde Park in support of Rohana Wijeweera, the leader of the movement. That day, I witnessed Wijeweera\u2019s powerful oratory for the first time\u2014magnetic and uncompromising. I glanced at Herath during the speech; his eyes reflected both intensity and hope, as if he truly believed a just Sri Lanka was within reach.\n\nH. B. Herath, a 1980 graduate of the University of Sri Jayewardenepura (photo was taken 1975)\n\nThe sustained protests eventually forced the UNP government to withdraw the White Paper.\n\nYears later, during my doctoral research in Sri Lanka between 1988 and 1989, I found myself once again amid turmoil\u2014the second JVP insurrection, known as Beeshanaya. It was a period of fear marked by targeted killings, attacks on infrastructure, and brutal counterinsurgency. An estimated 70,000 young people were killed or disappeared.\n\nDespite the tense atmosphere, I continued my field research in tea estates in the Nuwara Eliya and Ratnapura districts, where the violence felt somewhat distant. Still, the weight of that period stayed with me long after I returned to Minnesota.\n\nIt was later\u2014through BBC News, The New York Times, and other local media outlets\u2014that I learned of Herath\u2019s fate. He had been captured and mercilessly tortured by government forces in the dead of night. Nearly killed by prolonged torture, Herath\u2014still alive\u2014was incinerated along with the wounded Rohana Wijeweera. Each was executed a few minutes apart in the early hours of November 13, 1989. Like so many others, the 35-year-old Herath and the 46-year-old Wijeweera were extinguished prematurely in the shadows of a conflict that consumed an entire generation."
