    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "It was later\u2014through BBC News, The New York Times, and other local media outlets\u2014that I learned of Herath\u2019s fate. He had been captured and mercilessly tortured by government forces in the dead of night. Nearly killed by prolonged torture, Herath\u2014still alive\u2014was incinerated along with the wounded Rohana Wijeweera. Each was executed a few minutes apart in the early hours of November 13, 1989. Like so many others, the 35-year-old Herath and the 46-year-old Wijeweera were extinguished prematurely in the shadows of a conflict that consumed an entire generation.\n\nIn all this, Herath\u2019s dream of a young generation of rural youth who dared to aspire beyond their circumstances has finally become a reality. He surely believed that justice, dignity, and opportunity should not be reserved for the privileged few. In his own way, he carried forward the legacy of our ancient civilizations, where governance, however imperfect, aspired to serve the many.\n\nMy own path led me into academia, public service, and diplomacy. Yet it has been guided by the same ideals we once discussed under the shade of campus trees and across sports courts. If I have spoken for Sri Lanka on international platforms, it is also in memory of voices like Herath. His story is not just one of loss\u2014it is a call to remember and to act.\n\nAs Sri Lanka continues its search for reconciliation, justice, and equitable development, we must ask: how do we honor those who believed in a better future? Not through violence or division, but through service, integrity, and a renewed commitment to inclusive governance. That, perhaps, is the truest way to remember a sahodaraya.\n\nOn this 55th anniversary, I remember Herath not only as a fallen comrade, but as a forever hero\u2014whose dream of a just Sri Lanka lives on in those of us who continue the journey with humility and hope for a Renaissance Nation.\n\nDilmah Announces 2nd Annual One Earth Photography Competition to Inspire Conservation Visual Storytelling"
