    "md5": "c1e8942a78bcee1d4f1e6bb27cf737aa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1760,
    "chunk_text": "My first Aluth Avurudda in my brother-in-law\u2019s home\n\nListen to those bird calls. From among the usual sounds, a familiar but rare one seems to emerge now-a-days with increasing regularity \u2013 the call of the KOHA (Asian Koel) \u2013 bringing excitement and enthusing a weary nation.\n\nThis call signifies the arrival of the Sinhala and Tamil New Year \u2013 the historic National celebration of the Sri Lankans. Sleepy hamlets come alive as plans commence for the preparation of traditional sweetmeats for this event. \u201cAvurudu Games\u201d and competitions \u2013 both traditional and other \u2013 are revived, organized by villagers, groups and various associations.\n\nNew clothes are a must and sewing machines whirr. However, the most joyful event is the \u201cannual family get-together\u201d when family members dispersed over various parts of the country and the world come home to observe the rituals together at the given auspicious times.\n\nIn lighter vein, even the gambling followed by the drunken brawl come to have been accepted during Avurudu celebrations!!\n\nWhen I was just 12-years old we spent the New Year season in my brother-in-law\u2019s home in which he and my elder sister lived, plus the in-law family of parents and six siblings. Those girls and boys matched our family very well \u2013 two elder girls, two younger boys and two younger girls.\n\nThat was the first time I enjoyed a traditional village Avurudu celebration. One incident that is etched in my memory is how Ran Banda who wanted to show off his good looks climbed a jambu tree and started plucking the fruits for the girls. Then came a BRRRR sound and with the broken branch down came Ran Banda. He felt so awkward that he disappeared from the scene. Not truthfully, but politely everybody applauded."
